{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And he lodged</b> — he slept." "<b>His sleep</b> — sleep. And likewise in the Ishmaelite tongue." "<b>And anguish</b> — songs and melodies."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The Hebrew of the lemma is not visible in the provided text; 'ובת' appears to be a corrupted or abbreviated form, likely related to the Aramaic root for 'lodge/spend the night.' Translated hyper-literally based on Ibn Ezra's own gloss 'לן' (he slept).", 1 "'טות' is an Aramaic form from the Daniel verse; Ibn Ezra glosses it as 'צם,' likely a variant or shorthand for sleep/fleeing.", 2 "Ibn Ezra appears to interpret the Aramaic 'ודחון' (and anguish/fleeing) as related to music, possibly through a root association; the connection is left as he states it."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:20:14.324142641Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}